WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.627
Pandas gives us two important selection tools: loc and iloc.

00:00:04.627 --> 00:00:12.082
Loc is label-based. We normally use column names and, when needed, row labels or Boolean conditions.

00:00:12.082 --> 00:00:14.282
Iloc is position-based. It uses integer positions.

00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:17.882
In the first example, loc finds rows where channel equals web,

00:00:17.882 --> 00:00:24.147
then returns the order ID and amount columns.

00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:30.502
In the second example, iloc selects rows zero through one and columns zero through one using positions.

00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:33.112
Click Run and compare the results.

00:00:33.112 --> 00:00:40.672
For analytical code, loc is often easier to read because the code tells us what the columns mean.

00:00:40.672 --> 00:00:45.982
For example, selecting order ID and amount is much clearer to a future reader than selecting columns zero

00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:48.762
and two.

00:00:48.762 --> 00:00:54.657
Iloc is still useful when position itself matters, such as selecting the first few columns or working with

00:00:54.657 --> 00:00:57.107
a known table structure.

00:00:57.107 --> 00:01:04.290
The important distinction is simple: loc means labels and conditions; iloc means integer positions.
